/* ========================================
 * FEATURES: Watchlists
 * ========================================
 * Styles for the WatchlistsCollectionsScreen dashboard and panes.
 * ======================================== */

/* Overview dashboard grid and cards.
 *
 * TASK-998, second finding. The UAT called these "seven empty bordered
 * cards" and read it as a first-run problem. It is not: they were empty in
 * EVERY state, populated or not, and had been since the dashboard shipped.
 * Measured at 160x42 with a source present:
 *
 *     #overview-total-sources  region=Region(height=1)  content=Size(height=0)
 *
 * Two compounding causes, one per rule below.
 *
 * The grid had no `height`, so it took `Grid`'s `1fr` default and was handed
 * six rows for three rows of cards plus two gutters -- about one row each.
 * `height: auto` sizes it to the cards instead, which is the only way a fixed
 * card height means anything.
 *
 * And `padding: 1` inside `height: 4` with a `round` border left ZERO content
 * rows: 4 - 2 (border) - 2 (padding) = 0. Even given its four rows the card
 * could not have painted the label or the number. The padding is now
 * horizontal only, so the two lines the card actually renders
 * ("Total sources" / the value) have exactly the two rows they need.
 *
 * This is the same family as TASK-995 and task-875 elsewhere on this screen:
 * a container too short for its children, where the border is the only thing
 * that survives.
 */
#watchlists-overview-grid {
    layout: grid;
    grid-size: 3;
    grid-gutter: 1;
    height: auto;
    margin: 1 0;
}

.overview-card {
    height: 4;
    padding: 0 1;
    background: $surface;
    border: round $surface-lighten-1;
    text-align: center;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Failed runs table in overview */
#overview-failed-runs {
    height: 1fr;
    border: round $surface-lighten-1;
}

/* Empty state actions */
#wc-empty-actions {
    height: auto;
    margin: 1 0;
}

/* Section detail title */
#watchlists-detail-title {
    text-style: bold;
    color: $text;
}

/* Workbench: rails around a vertically stacked centre */
.watchlists-workbench {
    width: 100%;
    height: 1fr;
    min-height: 0;
}

.watchlists-region-left_rail {
    width: 28;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 100%;
    border: round $ds-grid-line;
}

/* Widened from 28 (Task 5): at 26 usable columns every Inspector action
 * label truncated -- "Stage Watchlists Cont", "Open current Watchlis",
 * "Console follow unavai" -- measured live at 235x52. 34 fits the longest
 * label ("Stage Watchlists Context in Console") close to on one line; the
 * `Button` rule below wraps as a fallback for whatever still does not fit,
 * rather than clipping it.
 *
 * Keeps its region border (Task 6, fix round 3): the ONE box per region is
 * drawn by the region wrapper, and the pane inside is stripped by ID below
 * (`#watchlists-inspector-pane`). See that rule for why the region, not the
 * pane, is the survivor.
 */
.watchlists-region-right_rail {
    width: 34;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 100%;
    border: round $ds-grid-line;
}

.watchlists-region-right_rail Button {
    width: 100%;
    text-wrap: wrap;
}

/* Collapsed breadcrumb levels (Task 5 fix round 2): muted and left-aligned
 * so they read as ancestry, not as an action -- distinct from the deepest
 * level's action buttons directly below them, which keep the default
 * centered/emphasized Button look.
 */
.inspector-breadcrumb {
    color: $ds-text-muted;
    text-align: left;
}

.watchlists-centre {
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 100%;
    min-height: 0;
}

/* FEEDS shared an equal `1fr` with ITEMS -- which hosts all six section
 * panes and their DataTables -- and CONTENT (Finding 5). At 160x42 that
 * handed FEEDS 11 rows for a handful of Static lines while ITEMS, doing all
 * the work, got the same 11. `height: auto` lets FEEDS take only what its
 * content needs so ITEMS/CONTENT split what is left.
 *
 * Task 5's premise that FEEDS "only ever hosts a short (<=5-line)
 * source-count summary" died in Task 3 when the section tab strip moved
 * into `_build_list_pane`: Task 7 gives FEEDS the real, scope-driven feeds
 * list, which can run to dozens of sources. Unbounded `height: auto` would
 * let that list grow without limit and push ITEMS/CONTENT clean out of the
 * viewport -- the opposite failure from the one this rebalance fixed.
 *
 * `max-height: 12` + `overflow-y: auto` caps it instead: FEEDS grows to fit
 * small lists, stops at the cap, and scrolls internally past it -- verified
 * scrollable, not clipped, with 40 synthetic rows
 * (Tests/Watchlists/test_watchlists_workbench.py).
 *
 * Fix round 1 shipped `max-height: 10`, one row BELOW the measured true
 * empty-state need of 11 (the tab strip, the "Sources" title, "No sources
 * yet.", and the Create/Import button row, inside the pane's own border) --
 * so even zero sources scrolled. That was wrong: a ceiling below the
 * content's own minimum is a clip with a scrollbar, not the intended
 * "grows to fit, caps, then scrolls" behaviour. Fix round 2 corrects it by
 * also giving ITEMS a bigger share than CONTENT (below: `2fr` vs `1fr`,
 * CONTENT is still the Phase-D placeholder stub and does not need equal
 * billing with the real list pane yet) rather than shrinking FEEDS further.
 *
 * With ITEMS/CONTENT at `2fr`/`1fr`, the invariant
 * (`test_watchlists_items_region_is_taller_than_feeds_region_when_expanded`,
 * unedited) needs `items > feeds` where, once FEEDS is forced to its cap by
 * overflowing content, `items ~= (budget - cap) * 2/3` for a fixed
 * `budget = feeds + items + content` (constant per viewport: measured 34
 * rows at the guard suite's 160x42, 44 at the app's real 235x52 -- both
 * confirmed by direct region-height measurement in a temporary pytest
 * addition, not assumed). Solving at 160x42: `items > cap` holds through
 * `cap = 13` (`items = 14`, margin 1) and breaks at `cap = 14`
 * (`items = 13 < feeds = 14`) -- confirmed empirically by forcing FEEDS's
 * real content to overflow 40 synthetic rows inside the full chrome-wrapped
 * screen (not just the standalone-workbench harness, which has a different,
 * more generous budget and would have understated how tight 160x42 actually
 * is).
 *
 * The cap is `12`, not that maximum of 13, deliberately. 13 holds only at
 * terminal height >= 42: at 160x41 the budget drops to 33 and 13 ties
 * (`items = 13`). 12 still gives `items = 14` at both budgets, so the
 * invariant survives a 41-row terminal -- a real size on a laptop with a
 * menu bar -- and survives any future change that consumes one more row
 * above the workbench. It clears the 11-row empty-state need with a row to
 * spare, so zero sources still does not scroll
 * (`Tests/UI/test_destination_visual_parity_correction.py::
 * test_watchlists_feeds_empty_state_fits_without_scrolling`). Taking the
 * maximum here would have bought one row of feed visibility for a boundary
 * that inverts on the next viewport down.
 *
 * Fix round 3 re-measured all of the above after moving the border from the
 * pane to the region (below): the numbers are unchanged, because the region
 * gains exactly the two rows the pane gives up. Re-swept at 160x42 with 40
 * sources: cap=12 -> items=14, cap=13 -> items=14, cap=14 -> items=13
 * (inverts). That constraint had no executable form until round 3 -- the
 * empty state never reaches the cap, so the older guard could not exercise
 * it. It is now pinned by
 * `test_watchlists_feeds_cap_keeps_items_taller_when_it_actually_binds`,
 * which forces the overflow inside the real screen; anything that consumes
 * another row above the workbench fails it rather than silently inverting.
 *
 * Keeps its region border (Task 6, fix round 3). Round 1 dropped it and let
 * the pane's own border stand instead, which was wrong three ways, all
 * measured: (a) region borders are `round` and pane borders are `solid`, so
 * the screen drew round corners on LEFT_RAIL/CONTENT and square corners on
 * FEEDS/ITEMS/RIGHT_RAIL; (b) `overflow-y: auto` scrolls this wrapper, and
 * the pane's border rows were *inside* the scrolled content -- at scroll top
 * the box's bottom edge was off-screen and at scroll end its top edge was,
 * so the one surviving border was an open-ended box; (c) the wrapper's
 * scrollbar painted in its own gutter, outside the box the pane drew. The
 * border belongs on the widget that scrolls.
 */
.watchlists-region-feeds {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    min-height: 0;
    max-height: 12;
    overflow-y: auto;
    border: round $ds-grid-line;
}

/* Companion fix for the region title's removal above: `#watchlists-list-pane`
 * (FEEDS's supplied pane) is one of a shared group of destination panes
 * given `height: 100%` by ID in `components/_agentic_terminal.tcss` -- an ID
 * selector, so `.destination-workbench-pane`'s own `height: 100%` was never
 * actually the rule in effect here, and no class-based override here could
 * outrank it (verified empirically: a descendant selector combining
 * `.watchlists-region-feeds` with `.destination-workbench-pane`, strictly
 * higher specificity than the plain class rule, still lost to the ID rule
 * above). `100%` of a region sized `height: auto` (FEEDS is
 * the only region styled that way) is circular -- the parent's size is
 * supposed to come FROM this child -- and before this task that circularity
 * was masked by the region title `Static` acting as a plain `height: 1`
 * sibling. With the title gone (a real content pane no longer needs a
 * second, wrong heading), the sole remaining child inherited the ambiguity
 * outright and Textual collapsed it to unreadable. This ID selector, same
 * specificity as the shared rule but declared later in the bundle (features/
 * loads after components/ in build_css.py), wins on source order and lets
 * the pane size to its own content instead.
 */
#watchlists-list-pane {
    height: auto;
}

/* Exactly one box per region (Task 6, fix round 3): the *region wrapper*
 * draws it and the pane inside draws none, rather than the other way round.
 *
 * These three ids are declared in `components/_agentic_terminal.tcss` (the
 * shared destination-pane block, ~line 3544) with `border: solid
 * $ds-grid-line`. An ID selector here matches that block's specificity
 * exactly and wins on source order -- `features/` loads after `components/`
 * in `build_css.py` -- so this narrows to Watchlists' three panes and
 * touches none of the other destinations that share that block. (The same
 * lever the `#watchlists-list-pane` height rule above already uses; an
 * earlier round wrongly believed stripping the border required editing the
 * shared `.destination-workbench-pane` class and thus 13 other screens.)
 *
 * Suppressing the PANE rather than the REGION is what makes the five region
 * boxes uniformly `round`, keeps FEEDS's border fixed while its content
 * scrolls beneath it, and puts FEEDS's scrollbar inside the box that owns
 * it -- see the note on `.watchlists-region-feeds` above for the three
 * defects the other direction produced.
 */
#watchlists-list-pane,
#watchlists-detail-pane,
#watchlists-inspector-pane {
    border: none;
}

/* ITEMS keeps its region border (Task 6, fix round 3) -- the pane inside is
 * stripped by the rule above.
 *
 * `2fr` (fix round 2, human-authorised lever change): previously an equal
 * `1fr` with CONTENT, which is what forced FEEDS's cap down to a value
 * below its own empty-state need in round 1 -- with an equal split, ITEMS
 * only ever gets half of whatever FEEDS leaves, so at 160x42 (34-row
 * budget) FEEDS=11 left 23, split 11/12, tying ITEMS instead of exceeding
 * it. Giving ITEMS a bigger share fixes this at its root.
 *
 * `2fr` is still correct after Task 4 wired the real reader into CONTENT
 * (below): CONTENT moved from a 1-row collapsed header to `auto` +
 * `max-height`, not to a competing `fr` share, so ITEMS remains the
 * `.watchlists-centre` Vertical's only `fr` sibling and this derivation
 * (and the FEEDS cap it depends on) did not need to change. See the note on
 * `.watchlists-region-content` below for why CONTENT is sized that way.
 */
.watchlists-region-items {
    width: 100%;
    height: 2fr;
    min-height: 0;
    border: round $ds-grid-line;
}

/* Task 4: CONTENT stopped being a collapsed 1-row header and became a real,
 * content-sized reader. It is deliberately NOT `1fr` (as an earlier version
 * of this task tried): `1fr` makes it a co-equal, ALWAYS-ON claimant on the
 * ITEMS/CONTENT split regardless of what it is actually showing, which
 * silently broke the Sources create-form fit
 * (`test_the_whole_create_form_fits_inside_the_sources_pane`,
 * `test_create_and_cancel_sit_side_by_side_like_the_dialog`) and the
 * FEEDS-cap invariant derivation
 * (`test_watchlists_feeds_cap_keeps_items_taller_when_it_actually_binds`) --
 * both tuned when CONTENT was a 1-row header and ITEMS got everything else.
 *
 * `auto` + `max-height` + `overflow-y: auto`, the same idiom FEEDS already
 * uses above, fixes this at the root instead of re-fighting the ITEMS split:
 * CONTENT is content-sized like FEEDS is, not budget-sized like ITEMS is.
 * With no item selected -- the default, and every state the create-form
 * tests exercise -- `ContentPane` composes one line of placeholder text
 * inside this border, so CONTENT naturally sits at its structural floor of
 * 4 rows (border 2 + the generic "Content" heading 1 + the placeholder
 * line 1 -- confirmed by direct measurement in
 * `test_watchlists_feeds_cap_keeps_items_taller_when_it_actually_binds`,
 * `content.region.height == 4`) and ITEMS gets back essentially all the
 * headroom it had when CONTENT was a header, restoring the original
 * `2fr`-is-the-sole-`fr`-sibling math the FEEDS-cap derivation above was
 * tuned against (CONTENT was never a genuine second `fr` participant there
 * either -- a 1-row `Button` isn't one). Once a real item is open, CONTENT
 * grows with its content up to the cap, then scrolls past it, giving actual
 * reading room instead of one line.
 *
 * No `min-height` here (fix round 2, coordinator review, Minor): an earlier
 * draft set `min-height: 3`, but the floor above (4 rows: border + heading +
 * placeholder) is never actually smaller than that -- there is no shorter
 * natural state for `auto` to fall back to -- so the rule never bound and
 * only misdescribed the real floor. Deleted rather than corrected to `4`,
 * since a `min-height` that merely restates what `auto` already produces
 * adds a number to keep in sync for no behavioural effect.
 */
.watchlists-region-content {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 12;
    overflow-y: auto;
    border: round $ds-grid-line;
}

/* Companion fix, same shape as `#watchlists-list-pane` above (same root
 * cause, different pane): `ContentPane` is a bare `Vertical` subclass with
 * no `DEFAULT_CSS` of its own, so it inherits Textual's built-in
 * `Vertical { height: 1fr; }` (`textual/containers.py`). An `auto`-sized
 * parent (`.watchlists-region-content` above) computes its own height from
 * its children's natural sizes, and a child that itself claims `1fr` (fill
 * whatever is available) is not a natural size at all -- verified
 * empirically: without this rule, `ContentPane` measured exactly the
 * parent's `max-height` cap (12) in every state, including the one-line
 * "Select an item to read it." placeholder, rather than shrinking to fit
 * its actual content. This override is what makes CONTENT small when idle
 * and lets it grow with real content up to the cap.
 */
#watchlists-content-pane {
    height: auto;
}

/* Solo must actually fill the centre (Task 6, fix round 3, Finding 3).
 *
 * `Z` (`action_solo_region` -> `RegionLayout.solo`) collapses the other two
 * centre regions to one-line headers. FEEDS is the only capped region, so a
 * soloed FEEDS stayed pinned at its `max-height` while the two collapsed
 * headers took one row each -- measured at 100x40 with 40 sources:
 * `feeds=13@y0, items=1@y13, content=1@y14`, leaving 25 of 40 centre rows
 * blank under a 13-row window scrolling 44 rows of content. Solo-ITEMS and
 * solo-CONTENT already filled correctly; only the capped region was
 * degenerate.
 *
 * The cap exists so a long feeds list cannot crowd ITEMS/CONTENT. When they
 * are collapsed there is nothing to crowd, so the cap is lifted (to the full
 * centre height) and the region takes the remaining space. `_region_widget`
 * adds `.watchlists-region-sole-centre` for exactly that state -- see its
 * comment for why it keys on "only expanded centre region" rather than on
 * `RegionLayout.solo_region`.
 *
 * Must stay AFTER `.watchlists-region-feeds` above: both are single-class
 * selectors, so this only wins the `height`/`max-height` tie on source
 * order.
 */
.watchlists-region-sole-centre {
    height: 1fr;
    max-height: 100%;
}

.watchlists-region-title {
    height: 1;
    text-style: bold;
    color: $ds-text-primary;
}

.watchlists-region-placeholder {
    color: $ds-text-muted;
}

/* Collapsed regions keep a one-line, focusable header so nothing vanishes.
 *
 * `width: 100%` is only correct when the header is a direct child of the
 * centre `Vertical` (`#wl-centre` / `.watchlists-centre`) -- there "100%"
 * means the centre column's own width. A rail header (LEFT_RAIL/RIGHT_RAIL)
 * is a direct child of the workbench `Horizontal` itself, so the same rule
 * there means the ENTIRE workbench width, pushing every region after it off
 * the right edge of the screen (Finding 1, fix round 2). Rails get a fixed
 * narrow width instead -- the same fixed-width-handle pattern
 * `console_rail_handle.py` uses for its collapsed rail buttons -- with
 * `min-width`/`max-width` pinned so Button's own `min-width: 16` default
 * (textual/widgets/_button.py) can't win the cascade and re-widen it.
 */
.watchlists-region-header {
    height: 1;
    min-height: 1;
    color: $ds-text-muted;
}

.watchlists-centre > .watchlists-region-header {
    width: 100%;
}

.watchlists-workbench > .watchlists-region-header {
    width: 16;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 16;
}

/* Section tab strip (Task 3, styled in Task 6 fix round 3 -- folded in by
 * the reviewer because the active section was invisible).
 *
 * `WatchlistsTabStrip.__init__` pins the strip to `height: 1`, and
 * `.watchlists-tab` carried no styling of its own, so the active tab picked
 * up the global `.is-active { border: round $ds-action-focus }`
 * (`components/_agentic_terminal.tcss`). A `round` border needs 2 lines
 * before it has a content line at all, so inside a 1-row strip the button
 * rendered as its own top border and nothing else: the active section's
 * LABEL never painted, which is the one thing the strip exists to show.
 *
 * Fixed here rather than in the shared sheet: that global rule is used by
 * many screens, and it is overridden the same way wherever a height-1
 * control meets it -- `#mcp-hub-rail Button.mcp-rail-row.is-active` and
 * `#mcp-mode-strip .mcp-mode-chip.is-active` in `_agentic_terminal.tcss`
 * both carry exactly this `border: none` + `text-style: bold underline`
 * pair. This mirrors the MCP mode strip, which is the same widget shape (a
 * one-row strip of mode chips), rather than inventing a new active look.
 */
.watchlists-tab-strip Button.watchlists-tab {
    width: auto;
    min-width: 10;
    height: 1;
    padding: 0 1;
    border: none;
}

.watchlists-tab-strip .watchlists-tab.is-active {
    border: none;
    text-style: bold underline;
}

/* Focus must not impersonate the active tab (bold underline above), and the
 * active tab must stay legible while focused -- same split the MCP mode
 * strip uses.
 */
.watchlists-tab-strip .watchlists-tab:focus,
.watchlists-tab-strip .watchlists-tab:hover:focus {
    background: $ds-focus-bg;
    color: $ds-focus-fg;
    text-style: bold;
    outline: none;
    border: none;
}

.watchlists-tab-strip .watchlists-tab.is-active:focus,
.watchlists-tab-strip .watchlists-tab.is-active:hover:focus {
    background: $ds-surface-raised;
    color: $ds-text-muted;
    text-style: bold underline;
    outline: none;
    border: none;
}

/* Tree node labels read as a list, so they align left (TASK-1091).
 *
 * Every node is a `Button`, and Textual centres a Button's label. With names
 * of different lengths that puts each one at a different indent:
 *
 *     ▸     Daily  0
 *     ▸  Security Watch  0
 *
 * The third Watchlists UAT read that leading gap as a stored leading space and
 * filed it as a trimming bug. It is not — `WatchlistBundleService.create`/
 * `rename` both strip before storing, and a whitespace-only name is already
 * rejected (verified directly against the service). The cause is label
 * centring, and left-alignment is the fix.
 *
 * Both properties are required. Textual's Button sets `text-align: center`
 * AND `content-align: center middle`; overriding only the first leaves the
 * label centred, which is what a first attempt at this did.
 */
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-root,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-watchlist,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-source {
    text-align: left;
    content-align: left middle;
}

/* Left-rail tree selection highlight (task-876).
 *
 * `WatchlistTree` rendered every node as a `compact=True` Button and never
 * read `tree_scope`, so nothing in the rail showed which node the centre
 * was scoped to -- the Feeds heading ("Feeds in Morning AI Brief (1)") was
 * the only feedback at all. This does not have the LabModeStrip/tab-strip
 * border-clipping defect -- `compact=True` already forces `border: none
 * !important` (Textual's own Button CSS), so the bundle's global
 * `.is-active { border: round $ds-action-focus; }` never gets a chance to
 * clip anything here. What it DOES need, same as every other `.is-active`
 * consumer in this file, is a legible look: the bare global rule only adds
 * `text-style: bold`, which is too subtle against three different node
 * classes sitting one row apart with no other affordance.
 *
 * Scoped with the `.watchlist-tree` ancestor class (matching the
 * `.watchlists-tab-strip .watchlists-tab.is-active` idiom just above)
 * purely for extra specificity headroom, not because it is required here.
 */
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-root.is-active,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-watchlist.is-active,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-source.is-active {
    border: none;
    background: $ds-focus-bg;
    color: $ds-focus-fg;
    text-style: bold;
}

/* Focus must not impersonate the active node (bold, no underline above):
 * the app's global `Button:focus` rule (components/_buttons.tcss) grants
 * `bold underline` to ANY focused button, active or not -- exactly the
 * ambiguity the Watchlists tab strip and Lab/MCP mode strips already guard
 * against. Kept a flat "bold, no underline" for the active node whether or
 * not it also holds focus (rather than the tab-strip's flip to a second
 * palette) since a tree node has no separate "resting active" appearance to
 * preserve.
 */
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-root.is-active:focus,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-watchlist.is-active:focus,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-source.is-active:focus,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-root.is-active:hover:focus,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-watchlist.is-active:hover:focus,
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-source.is-active:hover:focus {
    border: none;
    background: $ds-focus-bg;
    color: $ds-focus-fg;
    text-style: bold;
    outline: none;
}

/* The tree's write verbs (TASK-895): two one-row strips above the roots.
 *
 * `min-width: 0` is the load-bearing declaration. Textual's own Button CSS
 * pins `min-width: 16` (textual/widgets/_button.py) and `compact=True` only
 * removes the border, not that floor -- so three action buttons in a
 * `Horizontal` would claim 48 columns inside a rail whose interior is 26,
 * and the labels would render clipped with an ellipsis. That is not merely
 * ugly: `Tests/UI/test_destination_visual_parity_correction.py::
 * test_watchlists_left_rail_is_labelled_when_expanded` fails on any `…` in
 * the composited rail, which is how this was caught.
 *
 * At `width: auto` + `padding: 0 1` the two rows measure 21 and 20 columns
 * against 26 available, so both fit whole with the rail at its current
 * width of 28.
 *
 * `height: 1` on the strip matches every other one-row control group on
 * this screen (`.destination-filter-strip`, the tab strip): the rail is a
 * plain `Vertical`, so a strip left at `auto` would size to the tallest
 * child rather than to a row.
 */
.watchlist-tree-actions {
    width: 100%;
    height: 1;
}

.watchlist-tree-actions Button.watchlist-tree-action {
    width: auto;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 1;
    padding: 0 1;
    border: none;
}

/* Why the verbs above are off, when they are. Muted like
 * `.watchlists-region-placeholder`, and `height: auto` so the reason wraps
 * to as many rows as the 26-column rail needs rather than being clipped to
 * one -- a stated reason that does not fit is not a stated reason.
 */
.watchlist-tree-actions-note {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    color: $ds-text-muted;
}

/* The two prompts the write verbs need (TASK-895).
 *
 * Centred and sized, unlike the older OPML dialogs in the same module,
 * which are unstyled `ModalScreen`s and therefore fill the terminal from
 * the top-left corner. Type selectors so this reaches the modal screen
 * itself (`align` has to be set on the parent of the box being centred),
 * and the id rules below size the box.
 */
WatchlistNameDialog,
WatchlistSourcePickerDialog {
    align: center middle;
}

#watchlist-name-dialog,
#watchlist-add-source-dialog {
    width: 60;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 80%;
    padding: 1 2;
    background: $surface;
    border: round $ds-grid-line;
}

/* The rejection reason for an empty or duplicate name. `height: auto` so a
 * long name's message wraps instead of being clipped -- the whole point of
 * this line is that the user can read why nothing happened.
 */
#watchlist-name-error {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    color: $error;
}

#watchlist-add-source-options {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 16;
}

#watchlist-add-source-options Button {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 1;
    border: none;
    text-align: left;
}

/* One scoped source per row in FEEDS (Task 7, fix round 1, Finding 1).
 *
 * `height: 1` is the load-bearing part: `.watchlists-region-feeds` is capped
 * at `max-height: 12` and scrolls, and that cap was derived (see its own
 * comment above) against one-row children. A `Static` sizes to `auto` by
 * default, so a long feed name -- these come from remote titles and OPML
 * imports, not from us -- would wrap to two or three rows and silently eat
 * the region's budget. Pinning the row height keeps the list scanning as a
 * list and keeps the cap arithmetic true.
 *
 * `color` separates the rows from the scope heading above them
 * (`.watchlists-column-title`, bold primary) without a second border or an
 * indent, matching how `.watchlists-region-placeholder` sits under a title.
 */
.watchlist-feed-source-row {
    height: 1;
    width: 100%;
    color: $ds-text-muted;
}

/* TASK-998. The first-run panel that replaces the seven empty Overview cards.
 *
 * `height: auto` on both the wrapper and the body is the load-bearing pair.
 * `Vertical` defaults to `height: 1fr`, so the wrapper would claim the whole
 * region and re-create the "large box containing nothing" this task exists to
 * remove; and a `Static` left at its default single row would clip multi-
 * paragraph copy to its first line, which is the same class of defect as
 * TASK-995 one region over. `text-wrap: wrap` because this copy has to
 * survive the centre pane at 160 columns as well as at 235.
 *
 * No border. The region wrapper (`.watchlists-region-items`) already draws
 * one box, and Task 6's rule for this screen is one border and one heading
 * per region -- an inner box here would be the doubled-chrome defect that
 * task removed.
 */
#overview-first-run {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 0;
}

.watchlists-first-run-title {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    text-style: bold;
    color: $ds-text-primary;
}

.watchlists-first-run-body {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
    text-wrap: wrap;
    color: $ds-text-muted;
}

/* The Inspector's own first-run line, muted the same way, so the rail reads
 * as one voice with `.watchlists-region-placeholder` rather than as an
 * alert.
 */
#inspector-first-run-hint {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    text-wrap: wrap;
    color: $ds-text-muted;
}

/* TASK-997. The expand chevron drew on its own indented line ABOVE the
 * watchlist name:
 *
 *     │ Unassigned  0            │
 *     │       ▸                  │
 *     │ Morning AI Brief  0      │
 *
 * `_watchlist_node` now wraps the two buttons in this row so they share a
 * line. `height: 1` is what makes the row a row: `Horizontal` defaults to
 * `height: 1fr`, which inside the rail's `Vertical` would let one watchlist
 * claim whatever vertical space the tree had left.
 *
 * `min-width: 0` on the chevron is the other half of the original defect and
 * is load-bearing, exactly as it is for `.watchlist-tree-action` above:
 * Textual's Button CSS pins `min-width: 16` and `compact=True` removes only
 * the border, not that floor. A one-glyph chevron was therefore SIXTEEN
 * columns wide with the glyph centred in them -- which is why it painted
 * seven columns in from the left edge of a 26-column rail, reading as a
 * stray mark rather than as this watchlist's control.
 *
 * The name button is left at its natural `width: auto` rather than given
 * `1fr`: at `1fr` a name longer than the remaining rail would be ellipsized
 * (`test_watchlists_left_rail_is_labelled_when_expanded` fails on any `…`),
 * and the `is-active` highlight would stretch the full rail width instead of
 * hugging the label the way the sibling root nodes do.
 *
 * `width: 3` and not `2`, which is the tighter look this was first written
 * for: Textual's `Button` reserves a column each side via `line-pad: 1`, so
 * a 2-column button has ZERO content columns and Rich raises
 * `ValueError: range() arg 3 must not be zero` while wrapping the glyph. The
 * obvious `line-pad: 0` is not available -- Textual's integer property parser
 * rejects a literal 0 for it (`_process_integer` errors on `value == 0`), so
 * the stylesheet fails to parse and every rule after it is lost. 3 is the
 * narrowest width that renders.
 *
 * The source rows deliberately get NO rule: their two-space label indent
 * plus Textual's `min-width: 16` centring already places them past the
 * watchlist name (name at column 5, source at column 7 in a 26-column rail),
 * so the nesting reads without adding a margin that would only move with the
 * chevron's width.
 */
.watchlist-tree .watchlist-tree-row {
    width: 100%;
    height: 1;
}

.watchlist-tree Button.watchlist-tree-expander {
    width: 3;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 1;
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
}

/* TASK-897. `#sources-toolbar` is a bare `Vertical` wrapping several
 * `.destination-filter-strip` rows, and had no height rule anywhere -- so it
 * inherited Textual's `height: 1fr` default and its size came from whatever
 * its sibling `#sources-table` was not using, rather than from its own
 * controls.
 *
 * With rows in the table that looked fine (measured 160x42: toolbar 3, table
 * 13). With an EMPTY table -- the first thing a new user sees -- the table
 * collapsed to 1 row and the toolbar swallowed the rest: toolbar 15 of a
 * 16-row pane, and 25 of 26 at 235x52. Growing the terminal did not help,
 * because `1fr` grows with it.
 *
 * The four sibling toolbars (runs/items/rules/notifications) are `Horizontal`
 * with `.destination-filter-strip`, which pins `height: 1` in
 * layout/_panes.tcss, so they were never affected. This one is the only
 * wrapper, and so the only one that needed its own rule.
 */
#sources-toolbar {
    height: auto;
}

/* TASK-995. The Sources toolbar rendered as one bare `▊▔▔▔` bar with no
 * controls at all, which blocked the entire new-user path: create a
 * watchlist, press "Create source", and there is no visible way to add one.
 *
 * Two independent causes compounded, and both are fixed here.
 *
 * 1. HEIGHT. `.destination-filter-strip` is `height: 1`
 *    (layout/_panes.tcss) but a bordered `Input`/`Select` is three rows, so
 *    only the top border row survived. The widgets now pass `compact=True`
 *    (sources_pane.py / items_pane.py / watchlists_collections_screen.py),
 *    Textual's own supported one-row form -- the same idiom this codebase
 *    already uses ~300 times, including for `Input`/`Select` in
 *    mcp_server_mutations.py. That is done in Python rather than by
 *    un-pinning the strip: `.destination-filter-strip` is shared chrome
 *    (schedules, workflows and four sibling Watchlists toolbars), and the
 *    one-row strip is this screen's design language -- the tab strip, the
 *    tree action row and the header bar are all one row. Growing the strip
 *    to three rows instead would have cost the Sources table a quarter of
 *    its rows at 160x42, re-opening TASK-897.
 *
 * 2. WIDTH, which the strip's height alone would not have fixed. Nothing
 *    sized these controls, so `Input`'s Textual default (`width: 100%`) and
 *    `_conversations.tcss`'s bare global `Select { width: 100%; }` each
 *    claimed the FULL strip: measured at 160x42 the four of them stacked to
 *    x=31,122,213,304 in a pane 93 columns wide, putting `New Source` at
 *    x=395 -- 300 columns off the right edge of a 160-column terminal. The
 *    explicit widths below are what keeps every control on screen.
 *
 *    These must live in the bundle, not in a widget's `DEFAULT_CSS`:
 *    Textual always ranks a CSS_PATH rule above any `DEFAULT_CSS` rule
 *    regardless of specificity, so that bare `Select { width: 100% }` beats
 *    DEFAULT_CSS every time. Same lesson as `#mcp-tools-filter-server-slot
 *    Select` and `#mcp-audit-filter-*` in components/_agentic_terminal.tcss.
 *    `margin: 0` is part of that override too -- the same global rule adds
 *    `margin-bottom: 1`, which would push a one-row Select to two rows.
 *
 * Widths are sized to their longest option label plus the dropdown arrow
 * ("All statuses" 12, "Playlist"/"Inactive" 8), leaving the search box the
 * remainder: 19 columns at 160x42 and 94 at 235x52.
 */
#sources-toolbar Input,
#sources-toolbar Select,
#items-toolbar Input,
#items-toolbar Select,
#watchlists-header-bar Select {
    height: 1;
    min-height: 1;
    margin: 0;
}

#sources-search-input {
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 12;
}

#sources-type-select {
    width: 12;
}

#sources-status-filter {
    width: 16;
}

#sources-active-filter {
    width: 12;
}

#sources-tags-filter {
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 12;
}

/* TASK-1035. The create-source form had no rule anywhere, so it took
 * Textual's defaults for its container and painted like an unfinished
 * sketch: `Create` on row 40 and `Cancel` on row 43 with blank rows between
 * them, and `#sources-table` squeezed to a single row underneath.
 *
 * The container is now a `Vertical` rather than a bare `Grid` (see
 * `sources_pane.py`), which fixes the row spread; `height: auto` is what
 * stops it then claiming `1fr` of the pane and starving the table a second
 * way -- the same defect and the same one-line fix as `#sources-toolbar`
 * immediately above (TASK-897).
 */
#sources-create-form {
    height: auto;
    width: 100%;
}

/* TASK-1361. Name and URL share one row so the noise field below has rows to
 * occupy. Measured with the form open at 160x42: the Sources pane is 16 rows
 * (toolbar 2 + form 13 + table 1), i.e. the form had no spare row at all, and
 * a field of any height pushed `Create`/`Cancel` -- and then the table -- past
 * the pane's bottom edge, which is the unreachable-control defect TASK-1035
 * existed to fix. Pairing costs neither Input its border or its size, and it
 * is what this form already does for Type/Active and Tags/cadence.
 */
#sources-create-form .sources-create-identity-row {
    width: 100%;
    height: 3;
}

#sources-create-form #sources-create-name,
#sources-create-form #sources-create-url {
    width: 1fr;
    margin: 0;
}

/* Type + Active share one row. Two things are pinned here.
 *
 * The `Active` label was `width: 1fr` at Textual's default and claimed the
 * whole 168-column row, pushing the `Switch` to x=198 -- one column past
 * the pane's right edge at 235x52, so the only control for "is this source
 * active" was neither visible nor clickable.
 *
 * `margin: 0` on the Select undoes the bare global `Select { width: 100%;
 * margin-bottom: 1; }` in features/_conversations.tcss, which added a blank
 * row under the row and pushed the fields below it further down a pane that
 * has no rows to spare. Same override, same reason, as the toolbar block
 * above.
 */
#sources-create-form .sources-create-type-row {
    width: 100%;
    height: 3;
}

#sources-create-form #sources-create-type {
    width: 1fr;
    margin: 0;
}

#sources-create-form .sources-create-active-label {
    width: auto;
    height: 3;
    content-align: left middle;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* Tags + check cadence share the next row, for the reason spelled out above:
 * the Sources pane is 16 rows tall at 160x42 and a sixth full-height field row
 * would put `Create`/`Cancel` past its bottom edge. The Select needs the same
 * `margin: 0` override as the type Select -- the bare global
 * `Select { width: 100%; margin-bottom: 1; }` in features/_conversations.tcss
 * otherwise adds a blank row beneath it and reintroduces exactly that overflow.
 */
#sources-create-form .sources-create-tags-row {
    width: 100%;
    height: 1;
}

#sources-create-form #sources-create-tags {
    width: 1fr;
    margin: 0;
}

#sources-create-form #sources-create-frequency {
    width: 16;
    margin: 0;
}

/* TASK-1361 (spec §2): the noise control. `max-height: 4` is the whole field
 * -- two rows of selectors between its border rows, with the label on the top
 * border and the help copy on the bottom (see `_IGNORE_SELECTORS_LABEL` in
 * sources_pane.py). Two `Static` rows for that text is two rows this form does
 * not have, and a taller field puts `Create`/`Cancel` off the bottom of a
 * 16-row pane at 160x42. `height: auto` under the cap keeps it from claiming
 * `1fr` of the form the way TextArea's own default would, which is the same
 * defect and the same fix as `#sources-toolbar` and the form container above.
 */
#sources-create-form #sources-create-ignore-selectors {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 4;
    margin: 0;
}

/* `.dialog-buttons` (features/_media.tcss) supplies the side-by-side pair
 * itself -- `layout: horizontal`, and `margin: 0 1; min-width: 12` on each
 * Button -- so this form and `WatchlistNameDialog` pair their Create/Cancel
 * the same way rather than stacking them.
 *
 * Two properties are deliberately not inherited. The dialog centres its
 * pair inside a narrow modal; this form is inline and 168 columns wide,
 * where a centred pair would float away from the fields it belongs to. And
 * the dialog's `height: 3` would put the pair a row past the bottom of a
 * 16-row Sources pane at 160x42; one row is also what every other button on
 * this screen uses (`New Source`, `Preview`, `Import OPML`, the tree's
 * `New`), so the pair reads as part of the screen rather than a modal
 * dropped into it.
 */
#sources-create-form .sources-create-buttons {
    align: left middle;
    width: 100%;
    height: 1;
}

#items-search-input {
    width: 1fr;
    min-width: 12;
}

#items-status-select {
    width: 16;
}

#watchlists-backend-select {
    width: 12;
}

/* TASK-1105 lived here. Clicking any Watchlists table row other than the
 * first left the selection on row 0, so `Preview`, `Check now` and `Delete`
 * all acted on the wrong source. The cause is the app-wide keyboard-focus
 * fallback `*:focus { outline: solid $ds-focus-accent; }` (core/_reset.tcss):
 * Textual paints an `outline` OVER a widget's outermost rendered lines, and
 * the overwritten segments lose the `{"row", "column"}` metadata that
 * `DataTable._on_click` needs, so the bottom row of a FOCUSED table is
 * unclickable. The Sources table is three rows tall here (header + two rows),
 * which left row 0 as the only reachable data row.
 *
 * It was fixed with `.watchlists-region DataTable:focus { outline: none; }`
 * plus a `$ds-focus-bg`/`$ds-focus-fg` cursor recolour, scoped to this
 * screen, and its notes called out that every other table in the app still
 * had it. TASK-1160 promoted exactly those two rules to a bare
 * `DataTable:focus` type selector in components/_lists.tcss, which every
 * region here inherits unchanged, and removed this local copy so there is one
 * mechanism rather than two.
 * Tests/UI/test_watchlists_source_row_click_selects.py still guards the
 * Watchlists behaviour end to end.
 */

/* TASK-1020. The Overview region's and the Inspector's third state: the
 * overview worker has been started and has not answered yet. Muted like
 * `.watchlists-region-placeholder` and `.watchlists-first-run-body` so a
 * transient line does not read as an alert, and `height: auto` so it does not
 * claim the region's rows on the way past.
 */
.watchlists-loading-state {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    text-wrap: wrap;
    color: $ds-text-muted;
}
